{#
	What one number on this record has been worth, as a line (XIV-121).

	The card draws nothing at all for a module with no numeric field on it — see
	`RecordTrend::hasAnything()` for why that is nothing rather than an empty
	state. The record page mounts this unconditionally and lets the card decide,
	which is §8.3.1's split — the page decides whether a card exists, the card
	decides what is in it — applied one level down to a record instead of a
	dashboard.

	Every moment here goes through `|date`, which converts into the reader's own
	zone before formatting because a request-scoped listener sets Twig's timezone
	(§8.4.4). The chart's axis is formatted in the browser instead, by
	`assets/controllers/trend_chart_controller.js`, and both therefore say the
	same day.
#}
{% if not this.hasAnything %}
	{#
		Nothing to say, and an element to say it in.

		A live component is mounted on a root element and its props are written
		onto that element, so rendering literally nothing would leave the library
		with nowhere to put them. This is that element and no more: no card, no
		heading, no border, nothing that occupies a pixel. A record page for a
		module with no numbers on it looks exactly as it did before this feature
		existed, which is the point — an empty card saying "no chart" would be the
		feature refusing to leave (§5.18's rule about a panel, applied to a card).
	#}
	<div{{ attributes }} hidden></div>
{% else %}
	{% set trend = this.chosen %}
	{% set extent = trend.extent %}

	<div{{ attributes.defaults({class: 'card mb-3'}) }}>
		<div class="card-body">
			<div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2 justify-content-between align-items-center mb-3">
				<h2 class="h6 text-uppercase text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'module.trend'|trans }}</h2>

				{#
					Only when there is a choice to make. One numeric field is not a
					decision, and a select with a single option in it is a control that
					can do nothing — which reads as a control that is broken.

					`data-model` rather than a live action: the whole interaction is
					"this prop is now that", and the component re-renders itself. There
					is nothing to write down, nothing to validate and nothing to refuse,
					which is exactly the case a bound model is for.
				#}
				{% if this.trends|length > 1 %}
					<select class="form-select form-select-sm w-auto"
					        data-model="field"
					        aria-label="{{ 'module.trend_field'|trans }}">
						{% for key, option in this.trends %}
							<option value="{{ key }}"{{ key == trend.field.key ? ' selected' : '' }}>
								{{- option.field.label -}}
							</option>
						{% endfor %}
					</select>
				{% endif %}
			</div>

			{#
				The height belongs to the card rather than to Chart.js, which is why
				the options turn `maintainAspectRatio` off: left to itself it derives a
				height from the width, and this card sits in a column that is narrow on
				a laptop and full-width on a phone — so the same chart would be a
				letterbox on one and a square on the other. See `.trend-chart`.
			#}
			<div class="trend-chart">
				{#
					A canvas says nothing to a screen reader, so the label carries what
					the picture carries: which number this is and how it has moved. The
					sentence below repeats it in the page for everybody, because it is
					worth reading whether or not the chart drew.
				#}
				{{ render_chart(this.chart, {
					'role': 'img',
					'aria-label': trend.isFlat
						? 'module.trend_since'|trans({
							'%field%': trend.field.label,
							'%value%': display_stored(trend.field, trend.first.value),
							'%date%': trend.first.at|date('Y-m-d'),
						})
						: 'module.trend_summary'|trans({
							'%field%': trend.field.label,
							'%low%': display_stored(trend.field, extent[0]),
							'%high%': display_stored(trend.field, extent[1]),
							'%value%': display_stored(trend.field, trend.last.value),
						}),
				}) }}
			</div>

			{#
				What the line says, in words. Not a caption for the chart so much as
				the other half of it: a horizontal line and a chart that failed to
				load look alike at a glance, and "unchanged since March" is the
				sentence that tells them apart. It is also the whole answer for a
				record that has never been edited, which is most of them.
			#}
			<p class="small text-body-secondary mb-0 mt-2">
				{% if trend.isFlat %}
					{{ 'module.trend_since'|trans({
						'%field%': trend.field.label,
						'%value%': display_stored(trend.field, trend.first.value),
						'%date%': trend.first.at|date('Y-m-d'),
					}) }}
				{% else %}
					{{ 'module.history_changes'|trans({'%count%': trend.changes}) }}
					<span class="mx-1">·</span>
					{{ 'module.trend_range'|trans({
						'%low%': display_stored(trend.field, extent[0]),
						'%high%': display_stored(trend.field, extent[1]),
					}) }}
				{% endif %}

				{#
					The cap is the reader's business (§5.2's rule about a list that
					silently stopped). A line that began at whatever the oldest entry
					this read happened to say, presented without this, would be
					claiming the record was born at that value.
				#}
				{% if trend.truncated %}
					<span class="d-block">{{ 'module.trend_truncated'|trans }}</span>
				{% endif %}
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
{% endif %}
